-- Q1: easy / L1 — GDPR special categories of data (Article 9)
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Under GDPR Article 9, which type of data is classified as a special category requiring additional protections before processing?', '["Data revealing racial or ethnic origin", "Business email addresses", "IP addresses collected from website visitors", "Employee identification numbers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): GDPR Article 9 identifies special categories of personal data that require explicit consent or another specific legal basis. These include data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, genetic data, biometric data used for identification, health data, and data concerning sexual orientation.
Wrong (B): Business email addresses are ordinary personal data, not a special category under Article 9.
Wrong (C): IP addresses may constitute personal data but are not classified as a special category requiring additional protections.
Wrong (D): Employee identification numbers are standard personal data and do not fall within the Article 9 special categories.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — GDPR joint controllers arrangement analysis
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Beacon Health and NorthStar Analytics jointly operate a patient wellness platform. Beacon determines which patient data is collected, while NorthStar decides the analytical methods and retention periods. A data subject files a complaint alleging unlawful processing. Under GDPR Article 26, which analysis is correct regarding their liability?', '["Only Beacon is liable because it determines which data is collected from patients", "Both are joint controllers who must define responsibilities transparently, and the data subject may exercise rights against either controller", "NorthStar is solely liable because it determines the analytical methods and retention periods", "Neither entity is liable because shared responsibility dilutes individual accountability"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): GDPR Article 26 provides that where two or more controllers jointly determine the purposes and means of processing, they are joint controllers. They must establish a transparent arrangement defining their respective responsibilities, and the data subject may exercise rights against each controller regardless of the arrangement''s terms.
Wrong (A): Both entities determine aspects of purposes and means, making both joint controllers — liability is not limited to one party.
Wrong (C): NorthStar''s role in determining analytical methods and retention contributes to joint controllership but does not isolate liability to NorthStar alone.
Wrong (D): Joint controllership does not dilute responsibility; Article 26(3) explicitly allows data subjects to exercise rights against either or both controllers.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — HIPAA accounting of disclosures exceptions
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Riverdale Medical Center receives a patient''s request for an accounting of disclosures under HIPAA. Over the past year, the hospital disclosed the patient''s PHI for treatment consultations with a specialist, in response to a law enforcement subpoena, and to the patient''s own health plan for payment. The compliance officer must determine which disclosures must be included. Which evaluation is correct?', '["All three disclosures must be included in the accounting", "Only the treatment consultation must be included", "Only the law enforcement disclosure must be included in the accounting", "Both the treatment consultation and the payment disclosure must be included"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Under HIPAA''s accounting of disclosures requirement (45 CFR Section 164.528), covered entities must provide an accounting of certain disclosures made during the prior six years. However, disclosures for treatment, payment, and healthcare operations (TPO) are explicitly exempt. The law enforcement subpoena disclosure is not exempt and must be included.
Wrong (A): Disclosures for treatment and payment are exempt from the accounting requirement under Section 164.528(a)(1)(i).
Wrong (B): Treatment disclosures are specifically exempt from the accounting requirement.
Wrong (D): Both treatment and payment disclosures are exempt from the accounting of disclosures under HIPAA.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — CCPA definition of "sale" of personal information
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Redwood Digital, a California marketing firm subject to CCPA, shares customer browsing data with a third-party advertising network in exchange for discounted access to the network''s audience targeting tools. No money changes hands. The legal team must determine whether this arrangement constitutes a sale under CCPA. Which conclusion is correct?', '["It is not a sale because no monetary payment was received", "It is not a sale because browsing data is not personal information", "It is not a sale because advertising networks are service providers", "It qualifies as a sale because CCPA defines selling as disclosing personal information for monetary or other valuable consideration"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): CCPA Section 1798.140(ad) defines sale broadly as selling, renting, releasing, disclosing, or otherwise communicating a consumer''s personal information for monetary or other valuable consideration. The discounted tool access constitutes other valuable consideration, making this a sale.
Wrong (A): CCPA''s sale definition is not limited to monetary transactions — any valuable consideration qualifies.
Wrong (B): Browsing data linked to a consumer constitutes personal information under CCPA Section 1798.140(v).
Wrong (C): A third-party advertising network receiving data for its own purposes is not acting as a service provider under the CCPA definition.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — GDPR legitimate interest balancing test
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Crestview Insurance wants to use customer data to build fraud detection models under the legitimate interest lawful basis. Before proceeding, the data protection team conducts a balancing test. The team finds that the processing would significantly benefit the company but could result in unexpected profiling of customers with no reasonable expectation of such use. Under GDPR, what should the team conclude?', '["The legitimate interest basis fails the balancing test because the data subjects'' rights and expectations override the controller''s interest", "Legitimate interest automatically applies to all fraud prevention activities regardless of impact", "The balancing test is only required when processing special category data", "The company may proceed because commercial benefit always outweighs individual privacy concerns"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): Under GDPR Article 6(1)(f) and Recital 47, legitimate interest requires a three-part balancing test: identify the legitimate interest, demonstrate necessity, and balance it against the data subject''s rights, freedoms, and reasonable expectations. Where individuals would not reasonably expect the processing and it causes significant impact, the balance tips against the controller.
Wrong (B): Fraud prevention may support a legitimate interest, but it does not override the requirement to conduct and pass the balancing test in every case.
Wrong (C): The balancing test applies to all processing under legitimate interest, not only special category data, which cannot use legitimate interest at all under Article 9.
Wrong (D): GDPR does not subordinate individual rights to commercial benefit; the balancing test exists precisely to prevent this outcome.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — HIPAA designated record set
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Under HIPAA, what is a designated record set?', '["Any paper document stored in a healthcare facility regardless of its purpose", "Records maintained by a covered entity including medical, billing, and enrollment records used to make decisions about individuals", "The electronic backup copy of all patient data maintained at an offsite location", "A collection of de-identified health records that have been approved for research use"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Under 45 CFR Section 164.501, a designated record set is defined as a group of records maintained by or for a covered entity that includes medical records and billing records, health plan enrollment records, and other records used to make decisions about individuals. This is the set to which patients have access rights.
Wrong (A): Not all paper documents qualify — only those used to make decisions about individuals and maintained as part of specific record categories.
Wrong (C): An offsite electronic backup is not itself a designated record set; the definition focuses on records used for decision-making, not storage location.
Wrong (D): De-identified records are not part of the designated record set because they are no longer individually identifiable health information.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — GDPR children's data consent requirements
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'FunZone Games, a mobile game developer based in Berlin, collects personal data from users to personalize gameplay. The company discovers that 30% of its users are children under age 14. Under GDPR Article 8, the company must evaluate its consent practices. In Germany, the digital age of consent is set at 16. What must FunZone do to lawfully process these children''s data based on consent?', '["Nothing additional, because children under 14 can consent independently under GDPR", "Allow children to consent if they are at least 13, since that is the GDPR minimum age", "Obtain verifiable parental consent for users under 16, since Germany has set the age of consent at 16 as permitted by GDPR", "Refuse to process any data from children under 18"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): GDPR Article 8 sets a default age of 16 for valid consent for information society services, but allows member states to lower it to a minimum of 13. Germany maintains the default age of 16, so FunZone must obtain parental or guardian consent for users under 16. Reasonable verification efforts must be made.
Wrong (A): Children under 16 (in Germany) cannot independently consent to data processing for information society services under GDPR Article 8.
Wrong (B): While 13 is the minimum age member states may set, Germany has not lowered its threshold below 16, so the 13-year minimum does not apply there.
Wrong (D): GDPR does not require an age threshold of 18; member states set the threshold between 13 and 16.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — GDPR records of processing activities
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Under GDPR Article 30, what are controllers required to maintain regarding their processing activities?', '["Annual external audit reports on data processing", "A public-facing data processing dashboard", "Copies of all personal data processed during the year", "A written record of processing activities including purposes, data categories, recipients, and retention periods"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): GDPR Article 30 requires controllers to maintain a record of processing activities (ROPA) that includes the controller''s name, purposes of processing, categories of data subjects and personal data, categories of recipients, international transfers, retention periods, and a description of security measures.
Wrong (A): Article 30 requires internal records, not external audit reports — though audits may be conducted separately.
Wrong (B): The ROPA is an internal compliance document that must be made available to the supervisory authority on request, not a public dashboard.
Wrong (C): Article 30 requires documentation about processing activities, not copies of the personal data itself.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — HIPAA psychotherapy notes special protections
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Lakeview Behavioral Health receives a request from a patient''s insurance company for records to process a claim. The patient''s file includes clinical treatment notes in the medical record and separate psychotherapy notes maintained by the treating psychiatrist. The compliance officer must determine what can be disclosed for payment purposes. Under HIPAA, what is the correct approach?', '["Disclose both the clinical treatment notes and psychotherapy notes, since payment is a permitted use", "Withhold all records because insurance companies are not covered entities", "Disclose only the psychotherapy notes because they contain the most relevant clinical detail", "Disclose only the clinical treatment notes; psychotherapy notes require separate patient authorization even for payment purposes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): Under 45 CFR Section 164.508(a)(2), psychotherapy notes receive heightened protection and cannot be disclosed for payment purposes without specific patient authorization, even though the TPO exception applies to other medical records. Clinical treatment notes may be disclosed for payment without authorization.
Wrong (A): While clinical treatment notes may be disclosed for payment under the TPO exception, psychotherapy notes are explicitly excluded and require separate authorization.
Wrong (B): Insurance companies that are health plans are covered entities under HIPAA — withholding all records is not justified.
Wrong (C): Psychotherapy notes cannot be disclosed for payment without separate patient authorization, regardless of their clinical relevance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — CCPA authorized agent verification
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'A California consumer asks her attorney to submit a CCPA deletion request on her behalf. The attorney contacts Magnolia Retail, the business holding the consumer''s data. Under CCPA, what must Magnolia verify before processing this request?', '["Only the attorney''s bar membership", "That the consumer has authorized the agent to act on their behalf and verify the consumer''s identity", "Nothing, because attorneys are automatically authorized agents", "Only that the request was submitted in writing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Under CCPA regulations (11 CCR Section 7063), when a consumer uses an authorized agent, the business may require the agent to provide proof of the consumer''s signed authorization and may independently verify the consumer''s identity. Both elements ensure the request is legitimate.
Wrong (A): Bar membership alone does not satisfy the verification requirements — the business must confirm the consumer authorized the attorney to make the specific request.
Wrong (C): Attorneys are not automatically recognized as authorized agents under CCPA; written authorization from the consumer is still required.
Wrong (D): A written request is necessary but insufficient — the business must also verify the consumer''s identity and the agent''s authorization.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — GDPR data breach definition
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Orion Technologies experiences a server misconfiguration that makes its customer database temporarily inaccessible for 18 hours, though no data is accessed by unauthorized parties and no records are altered. The IT team restores access without data loss. Under GDPR, the data protection officer must determine whether this event constitutes a personal data breach. Which classification is correct?', '["It is not a breach because no data was accessed by unauthorized external parties", "It is not a breach because no data was permanently lost or modified in any way", "It is a personal data breach because GDPR''s definition includes loss of access to personal data, not only unauthorized disclosure", "It is only classified as a breach if the unavailability lasts longer than 72 hours"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Under GDPR Article 4(12), a personal data breach means a breach of security leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to personal data. A temporary loss of availability — even without unauthorized access or data alteration — qualifies as a breach because access to personal data was lost.
Wrong (A): GDPR''s breach definition encompasses availability breaches, not only confidentiality breaches involving unauthorized access.
Wrong (B): Permanent loss is not required; temporary unavailability of personal data constitutes a breach under GDPR''s broad definition.
Wrong (D): The 72-hour period is the notification deadline after discovering a breach, not a threshold for classifying an event as a breach.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — HIPAA marketing authorization requirement
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Meadowbrook Hospital wants to send patients promotional emails about a new weight loss program offered by a third-party wellness company. The wellness company will pay Meadowbrook a fee for each patient who enrolls. Under HIPAA, what must Meadowbrook obtain before sending these communications?', '["No patient authorization is needed because this qualifies as a healthcare communication", "Approval from the hospital''s institutional review board committee is sufficient", "A business associate agreement with the wellness company satisfies the requirement", "Written patient authorization disclosing that the hospital receives remuneration for the communication"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): Under HIPAA''s marketing provisions (45 CFR Section 164.508(a)(3)), when a covered entity receives financial remuneration from a third party for making a communication about that third party''s products or services, prior written patient authorization is required. The authorization must state that remuneration is involved.
Wrong (A): Communications promoting third-party products for financial remuneration are classified as marketing under HIPAA and require authorization.
Wrong (B): IRB approval relates to research activities, not marketing communications — HIPAA requires individual patient authorization.
Wrong (C): A BAA governs the handling of PHI by business associates, but it does not substitute for the patient authorization required for marketing communications involving remuneration.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — GDPR representative for non-EU controllers
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'CloudSync Corp, a data analytics company based in Singapore with no EU establishment, processes personal data of EU residents through its cloud platform. Under GDPR Article 27, CloudSync is subject to the regulation. What organizational obligation must CloudSync fulfill?', '["Appoint a representative established in an EU member state to act as a contact point for supervisory authorities and data subjects", "Register directly with every EU supervisory authority in each member state", "Establish a physical office within the EU before processing any data", "Obtain an adequacy decision from the European Commission for Singapore"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): GDPR Article 27 requires controllers or processors not established in the EU but subject to GDPR under Article 3(2) to designate a representative in writing in one of the member states where the data subjects are located. The representative serves as a contact point for supervisory authorities and data subjects.
Wrong (B): The controller must appoint one representative in the EU, not register separately with every supervisory authority in each member state.
Wrong (C): GDPR does not require a physical office — appointing a written representative satisfies the obligation under Article 27.
Wrong (D): Adequacy decisions are made by the European Commission for entire countries regarding data transfers, not obtained by individual companies to authorize their processing activities.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — CCPA financial incentive program evaluation
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Summit Grocers, a California retailer subject to CCPA, offers a loyalty program where customers share purchase history and receive a 15% discount on all items. Customers who opt out of data sharing lose the discount. A consumer advocacy group challenges the program. Summit argues the discount is a permissible financial incentive. Under CCPA, which analysis correctly evaluates this program?', '["The program is automatically prohibited because consumers lose a benefit when exercising their privacy rights", "The program is permitted if the discount is reasonably related to the data''s value, opt-in consent is obtained, and a financial incentive notice is provided", "Financial incentive programs involving data sharing are prohibited under CCPA regardless of their structure", "The program is permitted without restriction because loyalty programs are fully exempt from CCPA requirements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): CCPA Section 1798.125(b) permits financial incentive programs, including price or service differences, if they are reasonably related to the value of the consumer''s data. The business must provide a notice of financial incentive explaining the material terms, and the consumer must opt in. The program must not be unjust, unreasonable, or coercive.
Wrong (A): CCPA does not automatically prohibit all differential pricing — it allows financial incentives that meet specific requirements including proportionality and opt-in consent.
Wrong (C): Financial incentive programs are explicitly permitted under CCPA Section 1798.125(b), subject to specific conditions.
Wrong (D): Loyalty programs are not exempt from CCPA — they must comply with the financial incentive provisions including opt-in consent and proportionality.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — HIPAA de-identification Safe Harbor method
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Under HIPAA, the Safe Harbor method of de-identification requires removal of how many categories of identifiers from health information?', '["10 categories of identifiers", "14 categories of identifiers", "18 categories of identifiers", "22 categories of identifiers"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Under 45 CFR Section 164.514(b)(2), the Safe Harbor method requires the removal of 18 specific categories of identifiers, including names, geographic subdivisions smaller than a state, dates more specific than year, phone numbers, email addresses, Social Security numbers, and others.
Wrong (A): The Safe Harbor method requires removal of 18 categories, not 10 — this understates the breadth of the requirement.
Wrong (B): Fourteen categories is incorrect; HIPAA specifies 18 types of identifiers that must be removed or generalized under Safe Harbor.
Wrong (D): Twenty-two overstates the number; the Safe Harbor method lists exactly 18 categories of identifiers for removal.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — HIPAA permitted disclosures without authorization
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Pine Valley Clinic receives a court order requiring the production of a specific patient''s treatment records for a civil lawsuit. The patient has not signed an authorization for the disclosure. The clinic''s privacy officer must decide whether to comply. Under HIPAA, what is the correct course of action?', '["Refuse the disclosure because the patient has not provided authorization", "Request that the court withdraw the order because HIPAA supersedes court authority", "Disclose the patient''s entire medical record to fully comply with the court order", "Comply with the court order after ensuring the disclosure is limited to the PHI expressly described in the order"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): Under 45 CFR Section 164.512(e), HIPAA permits covered entities to disclose PHI in response to a court order without patient authorization. However, the disclosure must be limited to the PHI expressly described in the order — the minimum necessary standard applies.
Wrong (A): HIPAA explicitly permits disclosure in response to a court order without patient authorization under Section 164.512(e).
Wrong (B): HIPAA does not supersede court authority; it accommodates judicial process by permitting disclosures pursuant to court orders.
Wrong (C): Disclosing the entire medical record would violate the minimum necessary standard — only PHI specifically described in the court order should be disclosed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — GDPR data protection by default
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Pixel Media launches a new social platform where user accounts are created with all data-sharing features disabled by default. Users must actively enable each sharing option individually. Under GDPR Article 25(2), which principle does this default configuration implement?', '["Data protection by default", "Right to data portability", "The right to be forgotten", "Purpose limitation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): GDPR Article 25(2) requires data protection by default, meaning that by default only personal data necessary for each specific purpose is processed, and data is not made accessible to an indefinite number of persons without the individual''s intervention. Disabling sharing features by default exemplifies this principle.
Wrong (B): Right to data portability (Article 20) concerns receiving personal data in a machine-readable format, not default privacy settings.
Wrong (C): The right to erasure (Article 17) allows data subjects to request deletion and is not about default system configurations.
Wrong (D): Purpose limitation (Article 5(1)(b)) restricts processing to specified purposes, which is distinct from configuring privacy-protective defaults.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: hard / L3 — GDPR breach record-keeping obligations
(5, 'Privacy Requirements and Data Protection', 'Vanguard Logistics, a GDPR-subject freight company, experiences a minor data breach where an employee accidentally emails a shipment manifest containing five customers'' names and addresses to the wrong business partner. The DPO determines the breach is unlikely to result in risk to individuals'' rights and freedoms. Under GDPR, what are Vanguard''s obligations?', '["No obligations arise because the breach poses no risk to individuals", "Vanguard must document the breach internally including facts, effects, and remedial actions, even though supervisory notification is not required", "Vanguard must notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours regardless of the risk level assessed", "Vanguard must notify both the supervisory authority and all five affected individuals within 72 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): GDPR Article 33(5) requires controllers to document all personal data breaches regardless of severity, including the facts, effects, and remedial actions taken. This internal record must be maintained to enable supervisory authority verification. However, notification to the supervisory authority under Article 33(1) is only required when the breach is likely to result in a risk to individuals'' rights and freedoms.
Wrong (A): Even low-risk breaches must be internally documented under Article 33(5) — the documentation obligation applies to all breaches without exception.
Wrong (C): Notification to the supervisory authority is required only when the breach is likely to result in risk to rights and freedoms, not for every breach.
Wrong (D): Notification to affected individuals under Article 34 is required only when the breach is likely to result in a high risk to rights and freedoms, a higher threshold than supervisory authority notification.', 'hard', 3)
;

COMMIT;
